/tests/opencode-turns.test.sh\nsrc-tauri/src/dispatch.rs\nsrc-tauri/src/lib.rs\nsrc-tauri/src/mailbox_delivery.rs\nsrc-tauri/src/run/resume/orchestration.rs\nsrc-tauri/src/run_tauri.rs\nsrc-tauri/src/wake_coordinator.rs\nsrc-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs\nsrc-tauri/tests/wake_confirm_legacy_opencode.rs\nsrc-tauri/tests/wu_d_proactive_wake_integration.rs","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":14,"totalLines":14,"truncated":false}},"title":"planning/wakechain-gate/gates/touched-files.txt","time":{"start":1780923276566,"end":1780923276614}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_09b5e3a0e83f30fc016a26bb8c750c8198afe1dad897d1794d"}},"id":"prt_ea74c9d12001dLAX2oZnEqCkSG","sessionID":"ses_158b3e351ffePkuQnC9kglvomy","messageID":"msg_ea74c519b001TG0tTwbdm7Rl18"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780923276647,"sessionID":"ses_158b3e351ffePkuQnC9kglvomy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_wJ6JtyUgE7AZcRK4jKCrfmaB","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","offset":261,"limit":420},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n261: - Rule: an entrypoint that is reachable but undocumented is HIGH on `blast-radius`.\n262: \n263: ### Step 2.5.4 - Duplicate-systems inventory\n264: \n265: - Artifact: `research/NN-duplicates.md`\n266: - Role: identify other code paths that do the same thing as the touched surface. They may be in different languages. They may be older or newer. They may have diverged.\n267: - Rule: the inventory names each duplicate, its language, its current state (live, deprecated, dead), and how it differs from the touched surface (if at all). Diverged duplicates are HIGH on `duplicate-system-count`.\n268: - Rule: when duplicates exist, Phase 3 must explicitly address whether the WU cascades the change to all duplicates, consolidates them, or accepts the divergence. \"We'll deal with them later\" is not addressed.\n269: - Rule: this step uses the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) when the touched surface spans languages or when the duplicate inventory cannot be derived by simple grep.\n270: \n271: ### Step 2.5.5 - Cross-language trace\n272: \n273: - Artifact: `research/NN-cross-language-trace.md`\n274: - Role: trace the touched surface across language boundaries. Python → JSON → TypeScript. Shell env-var → PowerShell parser. File-write → file-read consumers in any language. API boundary → frontend consumer. Workflow YAML → script invocations.\n275: - Rule: dispatch the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) when the surface crosses any language boundary. The operator produces a graph of readers/callers/contracts.\n276: - Rule: implicit contracts across language boundaries are HIGH on `language-fragmentation`. Make them explicit, or score them HIGH and let Phase 3 narrow them.\n277: \n278: ### Step 2.5.6 - Risk profile (scored)\n279: \n280: - Artifact: `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`\n281: - Role: score each touched surface on the axes named in `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` (coverage gap, behavioral ambiguity, blast radius, language fragmentation, duplicate-system count, brittleness markers, change-path entropy, lifecycle visibility). Compute a per-surface verdict (LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH) and the rolled-up WU-level verdict.\n282: - Rule: every non-LOW score names evidence. A score without a path, symbol, or query a reader can verify is rejected.\n283: - Rule: scoring consumes prior halt records, `HaltRecord.component_candidates_considered`, `HaltRecord.residual_risk_refs`, and `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries when they affect any risk axis.\n284: - Rule: the WU-level verdict drives **pipeline mode** for downstream phases. HIGH → exhaustive mode. LOW → lean mode. MEDIUM → lean mode with the MEDIUM axes called out in Phase 3's proposal.\n285: - Rule: per-surface mode lets one WU run lean for one surface and exhaustive for another in the same ticket. Phase 3 onward read the per-surface mode, not just the rolled-up verdict.\n286: - Rule: the WU's risk profile is also aggregated into the project-level risk profile (`<project>/planning/risk-profile.md`) per the convention. This aggregation is what `~/ai/workflows/risk-reduction.md` consumes between tickets.\n287: \n288: ### Existing problem-map content\n289: \n290: - Artifact: `research/NN-problem-map.md`\n291: - Role: same as before — capture what exists now, what is already risky or brittle, which adjacent surfaces sit inside the blast radius, and which supported or user-reachable paths exercise this surface today.\n292: - Rule: if research already produced a draft `problem map`, use Phase 2.5 to validate and narrow that draft against the exact planned change surface. Do not keep a competing second map.\n293: - Rule: if research already produced a draft assumption register for the touched surface, carry it into Phase 3 for validation and narrowing into the approved assumption register in `proposals/NN-*.md`. Do not keep a competing second register.\n294: - Rule: keep the map bounded to touched-file/component ownership plus adjacent supported paths. Do not expand into a whole-system inventory, but do include pre-existing code-quality debt inside files/components the WU plans to touch so Phase 2.5 can estimate, own, or decompose it.\n295: - Rule: if the current supported path is unclear, return to research. Resume by updating the `problem map`, then re-enter Phase 2.5 before proposal work starts.\n296: \n297: ### Phase 2.5 mode and gating\n298: \n299: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the orchestrator classifies procedural vs new-value; only new-value questions reach the root.\n300: - Rule: bugs / ambiguities / drift / test-gap discoveries are handled per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Discoveries during Phase 2.5. New tracker tickets (JIRA or Linear, per project) that block the current WU halt the orchestrator until user disposition is given.\n301: - Rule: Phase 2.5 cannot run in lean mode itself — it is the input to mode selection for downstream phases. The artifacts must all be produced. The size of the artifact for a particular sub-step may be small (e.g. coverage inventory for a one-file change is short), but the artifact is required.\n302: - Gate: human. **This remains the first of the two surviving human gates.** The user confirms (a) the problem map names the right terrain, (b) the risk profile's per-surface verdicts are accepted, (c) any blocking-ticket disposition is settled, and (d) the WU is workable in the implementation pipeline at all (vs. needs deferral to a prototype) before proposal work begins.\n303: \n304: ### Defer to prototype\n305: \n306: Sometimes Phase 2.5 surfaces evidence that the WU is not workable in the implementation pipeline yet. Signals:\n307: \n308: - The risk profile rolls up HIGH on **most** surfaces (not one or two — most). Exhaustive mode would expand the WU into multiple WUs.\n309: - The duplicates inventory (sub-step 2.5.4) finds a sprawling parallel-systems landscape outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for one coherent WU, that the WU cannot navigate without architectural decisions or decomposition.\n310: - The lifecycle map (sub-step 2.5.2) cannot be drawn — too much of the touched process is operational knowledge the codebase doesn't capture.\n311: - The coverage inventory (sub-step 2.5.1) finds the touched surface so under-specified that characterization tests would themselves be a multi-WU effort before the actual change can be designed.\n312: - The cross-language trace (sub-step 2.5.5) reveals the surface crosses boundaries with implicit contracts in so many places that the change-path entropy alone scores HIGH.\n313: \n314: <!-- INTENTIONAL: \"defer to prototype\" is the user-visible Phase 2.5 disposition, not an unwired future-ticket stub; the implementation-pipeline orchestrator wires the dispatch, ticket marking, cross-link comment, and halt in its Phase 2.5 human-gate branch. -->\n315: When two or more of these fire, the Phase 2.5 human gate surfaces options: `proceed in exhaustive mode (the WU is just big)`, `defer to prototype (framing is too unclear; build a prototype to clarify, then re-ticket from its dossier)`, `terminate WU (the work is wrong-shaped; abandon)`. Choosing **defer to prototype** dispatches `~/ai/agents/prototype-orchestrator.md` per `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` with the WU's question as the prototype's question and the WU's `${ticket_id}` (`jira_issue_key` or `linear_issue_key`, per project) as `defer_source`. Before the implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU, it marks the original ticket as deferred with backend-specific evidence: the Linear path applies `deferred-to-prototype` through `linear-operator task=apply-labels create_missing=true replace=false`; the Jira path attempts `jira-operator task=transition target_status=Blocked`, and if `Blocked` is unavailable the backend fallback is a comment-only deferred marker. Sprint/cycle removal is attempted only where a declared backend operation exists; with the current Linear/Jira operator surfaces it is unsupported and recorded as `fallback:operationally-manual` in the immediate comment and execution evidence. The immediate comment cross-links the prototype identity or tracker key, the prototype dossier path or pending dossier path, the deferred marker, and the sprint/cycle removal fallback. When the prototype later publishes a prototype-test PR, spawned implementation tickets must inherit the prototype-test PR URL, branch, test paths/node IDs, marker reason, ticket mapping, and acceptance criterion from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`: remove the `prototype-pending:` markers, make the inherited prototype proof tests pass, and preserve original assertions unless a traceable strictly stronger equivalent supersession is recorded. The implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU; the prototype's dossier later ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n316: \n317: worked example: a WU defers at Phase 2.5, so the original Linear ticket receives the `deferred-to-prototype` label, or the original Jira ticket moves to `Blocked` with a comment-only backend fallback when that transition is unavailable. The cross-link comment names the prototype identity or tracker, records that sprint/cycle removal is unsupported through current operators unless a backend adds the operation, and points to the pending dossier. The prototype dossier ships with spawned tickets and an approved original-ticket disposition. If the dossier says `close-as-superseded`, prototype P4 files the spawned tickets first, then closes the original as superseded where the backend supports that status and writes backend-specific links/comments: Jira uses supported issue links such as `Cloners`, while Linear uses parent/sub-issue where possible or durable comments. If backend support is missing, the fallback is recorded rather than silently claiming the operation happened.\n318: \n319: Deferring to prototype is not failure. It is the recognition that the implementation pipeline's gates exist to enforce known structure on a clear plan, and the work currently has neither.\n320: \n321: ## Phase 3 - Proposal\n322: \n323: - Artifact: `proposals/NN-*.md`\n324: - Inputs: approved `problem map`, coverage inventory, lifecycle map, entrypoints, duplicates, cross-language trace, halt records and halt residual evidence when present, and **risk profile** from Phase 2.5; any research from Phases 1-2.\n325: - Role: propose design, scope, architecture, tradeoffs, `Forbidden behaviors`, a test-intent track, and the specific current-state risk the change reduces. `Forbidden behaviors` is behavior-forbidding only; it must not state work-narrowing exclusions that bypass touched-file/component ownership. **Do not** implement.\n326: - Rules: the proposal must be reviewable on its own terms. \"We will figure it out during implementation\" is not sufficient design. The proposer does not write its own risk reports.\n327: - Rule: **mode is per-surface, not per-ticket.** Read `risk/NN-risk-profile.md` and produce a per-surface mode list at the top of the proposal: which surfaces are HIGH (exhaustive content required), which are MEDIUM (lean with explicit MEDIUM-axis callouts), which are LOW (lean). Apply the artifact requirements named in `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode.\n328: - Rule: when the risk profile names duplicate systems on the touched surface, the proposal must explicitly address whether the WU cascades the change to all duplicates, consolidates them, or accepts the divergence. \"Cascade later\" is not addressed.\n329: - Rule: when approved Phase 2.5 inputs include `HaltRecord` evidence or halt residuals, the proposal must state whether it preserves, revises, invalidates, or pays down those prior residuals, including `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n330: - Rules: include a supported-surface track covering deployment mode, customer cohort, adjacent public or user-reachable paths, blast-radius notes for unchanged adjacent paths, migration path, rollback path, and observability.\n331: - Rules: include a qualitative net-value statement. State whether the proposal clearly reduces a concrete current-state risk on the current supported surface and whether that reduction clearly outweighs the added blast radius plus the migration and rollback burden.\n332: - Rules: name those inputs from the artifact fields. Existing-state risk comes from `known risky or brittle behavior already present` plus `current supported and user-reachable paths through the surface`; change blast radius comes from `adjacent surfaces within the blast radius` plus `adjacent public or user-reachable paths`; migration cost comes from `migration path` plus `rollback path`.\n333: - Rules: include a short assumption register. Each assumption must name the evidence it relies on and what source or observation would invalidate it. If research already drafted a register for this touched surface, Phase 3 validates and narrows it into the approved assumption register in `proposals/NN-*.md`. Do not keep a competing second register.\n334: - Rules: include a test-intent track. For each expected test or test group, name the change risk or verification risk, intended behavior or acceptance condition, selected level (`unit`, `component`, `particular-integration`, or `end-to-end`), fixture source or fixture application point, assumption-register link if the behavior depends on an assumption, expected observable signal, and any residual risk the test will not verify.\n335: - Rule: when the test-intent item is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, the test-intent track encodes lifecycle `WRITE` eval-spec authoring at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, not pytest test files or one-off verifier scripts.\n336: - Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype proof tests, the Phase 3 test-intent track must list each inherited prototype test path or node ID as a required test-intent item and carry forward the no-silent-drop / strictly stronger equivalent supersession rule from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`.\n337: - Rules: include a mandatory `## Proof plan` section with exactly these parse anchors:\n338:   - `**Runtime claim**: <one or more sentences naming the runtime behavior asserted by the fix>`\n339:   - `**Proof method**: <description of validation surface(s) that will exercise the runtime claim>`\n340:   - `**Evidence-class match**: <explicit statement that the proof method's evidence class matches the runtime claim's required evidence class>`\n341:   The proof plan is a proposal artifact, not a self-certification. Runtime-artifact-bound claims must name the runtime-artifact evidence class that will validate them; proxy evidence must be scoped to proxy-layer claims or paired with matching runtime-artifact proof.\n342: - Gate: no default human approval step. The proposal advances only by passing Phase 4.\n343: \n344: Phase 3 proposals MUST include a `## Estimate refinement` parse anchor with one fenced `yaml` block. The proposer's outputs contract includes `refined_story_point_estimate` and `estimate_delta_rationale`, and the fenced block is the only parse surface the orchestrator reads:\n345: \n346: ```yaml\n347: refined_story_point_estimate: 13\n348: estimate_delta_rationale: \"One sentence explaining the refinement.\"\n349: inherited_story_point_estimate: 5\n350: estimate_source: \"layer-3-slice\"\n351: estimate_delta_flag:\n352:   inherited: 5\n353:   refined: 13\n354:   over_2x: true\n355:   rationale: \"13 is greater than 2 * 5, so Phase 4 receives the delta as a scope-risk signal.\"\n356: ```\n357: \n358: After Phase 3 artifact verification and before Phase 4 prompt composition, the orchestrator dispatches `${ticket_operator}` with `task=update-estimate` and the parsed estimate inputs. The canonical prompt path is `${scratch_dir}/prompts/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.md`; the canonical log path is `${scratch_dir}/logs/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.log`. The Phase 4 scope-risk prompt receives `estimate_delta_flag`, including whether the refined value is `>2x` the inherited value (`true`, `false`, or `unknown`).\n359: \n360: ## Phase 4 - Risk Gates (required; apply-gate-set)\n361: \n362: Run the risk gate on the proposal artifact, the `problem map`, the supported-surface track, and the Phase 2.5 risk profile, not on hand-waved intent. The orchestrator composes one `apply-gate-set` prompt and dispatches `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` after Phase 3 proposal and estimate update, before Phase 5 hookpoint research.\n363: \n364: - Artifacts represented by the gate-set: `risk/NN-audit.md`, `risk/NN-scope.md`, `risk/NN-shortcut.md`, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, `risk/NN-proof-risk.md`, Phase 4 code-quality outputs, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, expected-process evidence, process-tree audit #1, and optional bootstrap-exception ratification.\n365: - `audit-risk`: presence, contracts, migrations, test-intent track, fixture source, residual-risk artifact, and other checklist obligations.\n366: - `scope-risk`: does the proposal stay within the stated scope.\n367: - `shortcut-risk`: do proposed shortcuts defeat the underlying purpose.\n368: - `supported-surface-risk`: does the proposal reduce risk on the current supported surface it claims to target, using the approved `problem map`, supported-surface track, net-value statement, assumption register, and **risk profile**, including adjacent public or user-reachable paths, deployment mode, migration, rollback, and observability.\n369: - `proof-risk`: does the proposal's `## Proof plan` map each runtime claim to a proof method whose evidence class exercises that claim. It remains a distinct row or an explicit ACR-285 inventory-resolution row with equivalent blocking semantics.\n370: - `code-quality`: Phase 4 pipeline-callable code-quality over current WU evidence remains part of the Phase 4 gate-set. Only `LOW` passes unless a valid bootstrap-exception row ratifies a non-LOW aggregate under `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Bootstrap exception`.\n371: - Rule: `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` writes or verifies the canonical Phase 4 join manifest at `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, projects expected-process evidence, and requires process-tree audit #1 evidence. Rows carry at least `canonical_output_path`, `size`, `mtime`, `sha256`, `verdict_line`, and `verified_at`, plus the operator schema's caller mode, raw/normalized verdict, applicability, currentness, ratification, inventory-resolution, and blocking-status fields. Currentness keys and row-level reuse follow `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification`.\n372: - Rule: the bootstrap-exception parser remains orchestrator-owned. The orchestrator parses the proposal `## Bootstrap exception declaration`, parses the matching DECISIONS heading and convention citation, and passes ratification evidence into the gate-set. The operator writes the row; it does not become the authority for the proposer/orchestrator four-condition declaration.\n373: - Rule: in **lean mode** (per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode) the supported-surface gate may pass at LOW with a single-paragraph supported-surface track and the audit gate may pass at LOW without per-AC residual entries. In **exhaustive mode** the gates require full evidence.\n374: - Rule: supported-surface termination is an orthogonal dimension from the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict. Evaluate it first and in this order: invalidated assumption that breaks the current problem framing -> return to research and resume at Phase 2.5; otherwise non-positive value on the current supported surface -> terminate the work. A `LOW` supported-surface verdict with a non-positive value signal still terminates. Only when no termination signal fires does the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict control the next step.\n375: - Rule: when no termination signal fires, any current, well-formed semantic `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` proposal-risk report means revise the proposal and rerun the Phase 4 gate-set from clean state. Do not keep old `LOW` reports after a substantive proposal revision.\n376: - Rule: preserve ACR-280 strategy selection. Supported-surface non-LOW records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_INWU` or `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_FOLLOWUP` when those classifications apply; scope decomposition records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SCOPE_DECOMPOSED`; code-quality non-LOW that is not ratified by bootstrap exception records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_INWU`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_HELPER_EXTRACTION`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_MOVE_AND_IMPORT`, in-place file-decomposition, or follow-up-ticket decomposition according to the current finding shape.\n377: - Rule: if proposal revision and risk review enter a second round, create or update audit history under `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`. Track prior-finding closure/regression, new findings, oscillation classification, decompose-trigger status, watch signals, and the current `continue` / `apply` / `decompose` determination.\n378: - Rule: if hard revise/review triggers do not decide `continue`, `apply`, or `decompose`, use the decision-agent dispatch pattern in `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md` before the orchestrator calls the loop.\n379: - Rule: if a proposer claims it \"re-ran the risks itself,\" ignore that claim and rerun or reverify through the actual Phase 4 gate-set.\n380: - Gate: model. The risk gate is the proposal review.\n381: \n382: ## Phase 4.5 - Alignment (optional; governance layer only)\n383: \n384: - Use only when the project explicitly has a governance or philosophy layer that needs a directional check.\n385: - Artifact: `alignment/NN-*.md`\n386: - Role: check the proposal against governance, patterns, and philosophy and return `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, or `NEEDS_REVISION`.\n387: - Rules: alignment is directional, not exhaustive. It does not replace the audit gate. If the proposal changes materially after alignment feedback, re-run Phase 4 as well.\n388: - Gate: model. `ALIGNED` is required when this phase is enabled.\n389: \n390: ## Phase 5 - Hookpoint Research\n391: \n392: - Run only after the proposal has cleared risk, and alignment if alignment applies.\n393: - Artifact: `research/NN-hookpoints.md`\n394: - Role: map the approved proposal onto the existing codebase and find reusable pieces, extension points, conflicting systems, and deletion candidates.\n395: - Rule: identify anything parallel that should be merged into or removed instead of re-created. The Phase 2.5 duplicates inventory (`research/NN-duplicates.md`) is the input — Phase 5 confirms whether the planned change cascades, consolidates, or diverges per the proposal's decision.\n396: - Rule: in **exhaustive mode** for any surface scored HIGH on language-fragmentation or change-path-entropy, dispatch the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) to produce the cross-language readers/callers/contracts graph for that surface. Hookpoints derived from a single-language grep are insufficient when the surface crosses boundaries.\n397: - Rule: in **lean mode**, the four required sections (reuse / extension / conflicting / deletion candidates) are required as headers; sections may be one line or \"none observed\" but must be present.\n398: - Rule: All implementation branch work follows `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallelism does not change the requirement.\n399: - Rule: if hookpoint research shows the approved `problem map` or assumption register is wrong, stop and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 with an updated `problem map` before implementation continues. Returning to Phase 2.5 re-engages the problem-map human gate.\n400: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the orchestrator classifies procedural vs new-value; only new-value questions reach the root.\n401: - Gate: model. The orchestrator advances autonomously to Phase 6 once the hookpoints artifact is non-empty, contains the four required sections (reuse points / extension points / conflicting systems / deletion candidates), and does not invalidate the approved problem map or assumption register.\n402: \n403: ## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n404: \n405: Implementation has three sub-steps.\n406: The test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\n407: That rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\n408: Step 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\n409: When Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\n410: Rule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\n411: After Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n412: \n413: - Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n414: - Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n415: - Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n416: - Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n417: - Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n418: - Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n419: - Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n420: - Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n421: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n422: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n423: - Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n424: - Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n425: \n426: ### Step 6a - Define contract\n427: \n428: - Owner: orchestrator\n429: - Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n430: - Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n431: - Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n432: - Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n433: - Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n434:   1. `parent_level_id`\n435:   2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n436:   3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n437:   4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n438:   5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n439:   6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n440:   7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n441:   8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n442: - Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n443: - Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n444: \n445: ### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n446: \n447: - Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n448: - Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n449: - Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n450: - Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n451: - Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n452: - Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n453: - Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n454: - Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n455: - Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n456: - Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n457: - Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n458: - Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n459: - Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n460: - Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n461: - Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n462: - Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n463: - Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n464: - Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n465: - Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n466: - Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n467: - Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n468: - Output: `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n469: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n470: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype-risk verdict semantics. Only `LOW` advances Phase 6 prototype risk to downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord` production or consumption, or Phase 7 advance. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` model verdicts block advance, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n471: - Rule: Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review consumes the Step 6a contract, Step 6b tests or structural-verification eval specs, and `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; checks them against the approved proposal test-intent track before Step 6c; and writes `${planning_dir}/alignment/${wu_lower}-tests-contracts.md` with verdict vocabulary `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, and `NEEDS_REVISION`. The workflow doc is the source of truth for this model-gate review's inputs, artifact path, and verdict vocabulary; the dispatch, artifact check, verdict check, refusal behavior, audit-history append, and allow-advance condition live in the Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review sub-step in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`.\n472: \n473: ### Step 6c - Write code\n474: \n475: - Inputs: contracts, `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, and the tests or structural-verification eval specs from Step 6b.\n476: - Rule: the code writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6b.\n477: - Rule: the job is to make the tests pass while respecting the approved design and architecture.\n478: - Rule: If implementation reveals a procedural obligation in races, ordering constraints, retries, resource lifecycle quirks, behaviors-under-conditions, or implementation-specific quirks that is not already represented by Step 6b tests, the code writer MUST record the obligation and dispatch a procedural-test handoff.\n479: - Rule: Recording requires writing the procedural obligation and the Step 6c evidence that discovered it to `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`. The handoff dispatches a fresh, separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation; the Step 6c code writer MUST NOT author the procedural test inline.\n480: - Rule: if tests fail after Step 6c, re-invoke the code agent with the test output.\n481: - Rule: do **not** re-invoke the test agent because the implementation failed.\n482: - Rule: if a test is wrong, that is the contract's fault, not the test's.\n483: - Rule: if contract intent truly changed, revise the contract explicitly and regenerate the affected tests from that revised contract.\n484: - Rule: Recursive Step 6c consumes the Step 6b output index at `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, emitted tests or structural-verification eval specs, test/eval paths, and test/eval-group identifiers for the matching `level_id`. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, the orchestrator calls `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` before Step 6c dispatch, and the expected-process manifest carries the side-file evidence for the scoped Step 6b output index and each implemented Step 6b output-index row. Child rows must preserve `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` scoping where the index uses string artifact identifiers.\n485: - Rule: The component cannot close until each implementation-discovered procedural obligation has either an authored procedural test linked from the Step 6b output index, or a recorded residual entry with residual class.\n486: - Operational note: implementation happens in worktrees per `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallel implementation additionally requires disjoint worktrees.\n487: - Rule: Step 6c consumption evidence for ACR-247 side-channel runs is the manifest-declared side-file projected before dispatch by `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md`. The Step 6c prompt and log remain companion topology evidence, but model-authored `consumed:` rows, read-confirmation rows, or equivalent attestations are not load-bearing firstness evidence. Missing, malformed, contradicted, stale, unmapped, late, or wrong-scope side-file evidence does not satisfy Phase 6 firstness evidence.\n488: - Rule: an implementation that inherited prototype proof tests is incomplete while those tests are missing from the diff or output index, still marked `prototype-pending:`, unrun, failing, or replaced without a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index.\n489: - Rule: per-component code-quality auditor fanout is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` for each individual component after that component's Step 6c implementation and scoped tests pass, using `component_slug` to preserve `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/dispatch-manifest.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/cohesion-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/function-classification-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/push-pull-auditor.md`, and `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/aggregate-code-quality.md`. The gate-set dispatches or verifies the whole touched component rows before that component closes into the aggregate diff consumed by downstream review. This per-component fanout does NOT replace Phase 8 PR-review aggregate gates.\n490: - Rule: Phase 6 per-component code-quality auditors run outside the worktree. The gate-set computes or consumes `code_quality_dispatch_dir` as the nearest existing common ancestor of absolute `worktree_path` and `planning_dir`, then dispatches each code-quality child with `agents -m <model> -p ${code_quality_dispatch_dir} -f <prompt>`. Each child prompt must pass absolute `worktree_path`, `contract_path=${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md`, and `proposal_path=${planning_dir}/proposals/${wu_lower}-${wu_id}.md`. Every selected child auditor must read the Step 6a contract before scoring declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, proof, or validation context; missing or unreadable contract evidence is `BLOCKED`, not generic scoring fallback.\n491: - Rule: per-component code-quality verdict semantics. The per-component code-quality auditor fanout is pipeline-callable and blocking. Only `LOW` per-component aggregate verdict allows that component to close into the aggregate diff consumed by Phase 7 CodeRabbit. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` per-component verdicts block component closure, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging remediation rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n492: - Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation: after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype that passes level behavior tests, derive internal component contracts only when Phase 3 opened recursive or component-decomposition scope, or when candidate internal components emerge from the passing prototype.\n493: - Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation is precisely one layer deep at the current recursion level. The derivation output may name only the immediate internal components needed to satisfy what the passing prototype found at that level; it must not include nested sub-components, grandchild components, or any multi-layer component hierarchy in the same derivation pass. Deeper decomposition happens only through recursive child entry and recursive Phase 6 reuse after an accepted immediate component opens a child level with its own `level_id`.\n494: - Rule: Halt with `multi_layer_derivation_violation` when a Step 6c derivation output includes more than one component depth layer in a single derivation pass. The current level must not hand multi-layer derivation output forward as valid Phase 7 or child-recursion evidence. Orchestrator-runtime detection and emission live in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` Step 6c (the multi-layer derivation acceptance check).\n495: - Rule: when neither trigger arm fires, no derivation record is required and Phase 7 proceeds unchanged.\n496: - Rule: when either trigger arm fires and no internal component split is accepted, record an evidence-bearing no-split / rejection subsection before Phase 7. When halt logic fires, that subsection is the `HaltRecord` named below.\n497: - Rule: for docs/workflow WUs whose guard is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, regression-guard responsibility routes actively through WRITE eval-spec authoring under `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`; Step 6b must not add markdown structural shape tests, pytest-shaped assertions, or one-off verifier scripts as behavior evidence.\n498: - Rule: derived internal contracts are recorded as named post-prototype subsections in the existing `${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md` contract artifact, not in a new file.\n499: - Rule: derivation-record fields are `prototype_evidence_links`, `accidental_coupling_exclusions`, `neighbor_claims`, `rejected_component_candidates`, `generalization_notes`, and `generalization_probe_refs`; `generalization_probe_refs` records divergence references only and does not specify probe content in this workflow.\n500: - Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n501: - Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n502: - Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n503: - Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n504: - Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 261-504. Use offset=505 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- Rule: an entrypoint that is reachable but undocumented is HIGH on `blast-radius`.\n\n### Step 2.5.4 - Duplicate-systems inventory\n\n- Artifact: `research/NN-duplicates.md`\n- Role: identify other code paths that do the same thing as the touched surface. They may be in different languages. They may be older or newer. They may have diverged.\n- Rule: the inventory names each duplicate, its language, its current state (live, deprecated, dead), and how it differs from the touched surface (if at all). Diverged duplicates are HIGH on `duplicate-system-count`.\n- Rule: when duplicates exist, Phase 3 must explicitly address whether the WU cascades the change to all duplicates, consolidates them, or accepts the divergence. \"We'll deal with them later\" is not addressed.\n- Rule: this step uses the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) when the touched surface spans languages or when the duplicate inventory cannot be derived by simple grep.\n\n### Step 2.5.5 - Cross-language trace\n\n- Artifact: `research/NN-cross-language-trace.md`\n- Role: trace the touched surface across language boundaries. Python → JSON → TypeScript. Shell env-var → PowerShell parser. File-write → file-read consumers in any language. API boundary → frontend consumer. Workflow YAML → script invocations.\n- Rule: dispatch the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) when the surface crosses any language boundary. The operator produces a graph of readers/callers/contracts.\n- Rule: implicit contracts across language boundaries are HIGH on `language-fragmentation`. Make them explicit, or score them HIGH and let Phase 3 narrow them.\n\n### Step 2.5.6 - Risk profile (scored)\n\n- Artifact: `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","text":"- Rule: an entrypoint that is reachable but undocumented is HIGH on `blast-radius`.\n\n### Step 2.5.4 - Duplicate-systems inventory\n\n- Artifact: `research/NN-duplicates.md`\n- Role: identify other code paths that do the same thing as the touched surface. They may be in different languages. They may be older or newer. They may have diverged.\n- Rule: the inventory names each duplicate, its language, its current state (live, deprecated, dead), and how it differs from the touched surface (if at all). Diverged duplicates are HIGH on `duplicate-system-count`.\n- Rule: when duplicates exist, Phase 3 must explicitly address whether the WU cascades the change to all duplicates, consolidates them, or accepts the divergence. \"We'll deal with them later\" is not addressed.\n- Rule: this step uses the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) when the touched surface spans languages or when the duplicate inventory cannot be derived by simple grep.\n\n### Step 2.5.5 - Cross-language trace\n\n- Artifact: `research/NN-cross-language-trace.md`\n- Role: trace the touched surface across language boundaries. Python → JSON → TypeScript. Shell env-var → PowerShell parser. File-write → file-read consumers in any language. API boundary → frontend consumer. Workflow YAML → script invocations.\n- Rule: dispatch the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) when the surface crosses any language boundary. The operator produces a graph of readers/callers/contracts.\n- Rule: implicit contracts across language boundaries are HIGH on `language-fragmentation`. Make them explicit, or score them HIGH and let Phase 3 narrow them.\n\n### Step 2.5.6 - Risk profile (scored)\n\n- Artifact: `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`\n- Role: score each touched surface on the axes named in `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` (coverage gap, behavioral ambiguity, blast radius, language fragmentation, duplicate-system count, brittleness markers, change-path entropy, lifecycle visibility). Compute a per-surface verdict (LOW / MEDIUM / HIGH) and the rolled-up WU-level verdict.\n- Rule: every non-LOW score names evidence. A score without a path, symbol, or query a reader can verify is rejected.\n- Rule: scoring consumes prior halt records, `HaltRecord.component_candidates_considered`, `HaltRecord.residual_risk_refs`, and `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries when they affect any risk axis.\n- Rule: the WU-level verdict drives **pipeline mode** for downstream phases. HIGH → exhaustive mode. LOW → lean mode. MEDIUM → lean mode with the MEDIUM axes called out in Phase 3's proposal.\n- Rule: per-surface mode lets one WU run lean for one surface and exhaustive for another in the same ticket. Phase 3 onward read the per-surface mode, not just the rolled-up verdict.\n- Rule: the WU's risk profile is also aggregated into the project-level risk profile (`<project>/planning/risk-profile.md`) per the convention. This aggregation is what `~/ai/workflows/risk-reduction.md` consumes between tickets.\n\n### Existing problem-map content\n\n- Artifact: `research/NN-problem-map.md`\n- Role: same as before — capture what exists now, what is already risky or brittle, which adjacent surfaces sit inside the blast radius, and which supported or user-reachable paths exercise this surface today.\n- Rule: if research already produced a draft `problem map`, use Phase 2.5 to validate and narrow that draft against the exact planned change surface. Do not keep a competing second map.\n- Rule: if research already produced a draft assumption register for the touched surface, carry it into Phase 3 for validation and narrowing into the approved assumption register in `proposals/NN-*.md`. Do not keep a competing second register.\n- Rule: keep the map bounded to touched-file/component ownership plus adjacent supported paths. Do not expand into a whole-system inventory, but do include pre-existing code-quality debt inside files/components the WU plans to touch so Phase 2.5 can estimate, own, or decompose it.\n- Rule: if the current supported path is unclear, return to research. Resume by updating the `problem map`, then re-enter Phase 2.5 before proposal work starts.\n\n### Phase 2.5 mode and gating\n\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the orchestrator classifies procedural vs new-value; only new-value questions reach the root.\n- Rule: bugs / ambiguities / drift / test-gap discoveries are handled per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Discoveries during Phase 2.5. New tracker tickets (JIRA or Linear, per project) that block the current WU halt the orchestrator until user disposition is given.\n- Rule: Phase 2.5 cannot run in lean mode itself — it is the input to mode selection for downstream phases. The artifacts must all be produced. The size of the artifact for a particular sub-step may be small (e.g. coverage inventory for a one-file change is short), but the artifact is required.\n- Gate: human. **This remains the first of the two surviving human gates.** The user confirms (a) the problem map names the right terrain, (b) the risk profile's per-surface verdicts are accepted, (c) any blocking-ticket disposition is settled, and (d) the WU is workable in the implementation pipeline at all (vs. needs deferral to a prototype) before proposal work begins.\n\n### Defer to prototype\n\nSometimes Phase 2.5 surfaces evidence that the WU is not workable in the implementation pipeline yet. Signals:\n\n- The risk profile rolls up HIGH on **most** surfaces (not one or two — most). Exhaustive mode would expand the WU into multiple WUs.\n- The duplicates inventory (sub-step 2.5.4) finds a sprawling parallel-systems landscape outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for one coherent WU, that the WU cannot navigate without architectural decisions or decomposition.\n- The lifecycle map (sub-step 2.5.2) cannot be drawn — too much of the touched process is operational knowledge the codebase doesn't capture.\n- The coverage inventory (sub-step 2.5.1) finds the touched surface so under-specified that characterization tests would themselves be a multi-WU effort before the actual change can be designed.\n- The cross-language trace (sub-step 2.5.5) reveals the surface crosses boundaries with implicit contracts in so many places that the change-path entropy alone scores HIGH.\n\n<!-- INTENTIONAL: \"defer to prototype\" is the user-visible Phase 2.5 disposition, not an unwired future-ticket stub; the implementation-pipeline orchestrator wires the dispatch, ticket marking, cross-link comment, and halt in its Phase 2.5 human-gate branch. -->\nWhen two or more of these fire, the Phase 2.5 human gate surfaces options: `proceed in exhaustive mode (the WU is just big)`, `defer to prototype (framing is too unclear; build a prototype to clarify, then re-ticket from its dossier)`, `terminate WU (the work is wrong-shaped; abandon)`. Choosing **defer to prototype** dispatches `~/ai/agents/prototype-orchestrator.md` per `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` with the WU's question as the prototype's question and the WU's `${ticket_id}` (`jira_issue_key` or `linear_issue_key`, per project) as `defer_source`. Before the implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU, it marks the original ticket as deferred with backend-specific evidence: the Linear path applies `deferred-to-prototype` through `linear-operator task=apply-labels create_missing=true replace=false`; the Jira path attempts `jira-operator task=transition target_status=Blocked`, and if `Blocked` is unavailable the backend fallback is a comment-only deferred marker. Sprint/cycle removal is attempted only where a declared backend operation exists; with the current Linear/Jira operator surfaces it is unsupported and recorded as `fallback:operationally-manual` in the immediate comment and execution evidence. The immediate comment cross-links the prototype identity or tracker key, the prototype dossier path or pending dossier path, the deferred marker, and the sprint/cycle removal fallback. When the prototype later publishes a prototype-test PR, spawned implementation tickets must inherit the prototype-test PR URL, branch, test paths/node IDs, marker reason, ticket mapping, and acceptance criterion from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`: remove the `prototype-pending:` markers, make the inherited prototype proof tests pass, and preserve original assertions unless a traceable strictly stronger equivalent supersession is recorded. The implementation orchestrator halts the deferred WU; the prototype's dossier later ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\nworked example: a WU defers at Phase 2.5, so the original Linear ticket receives the `deferred-to-prototype` label, or the original Jira ticket moves to `Blocked` with a comment-only backend fallback when that transition is unavailable. The cross-link comment names the prototype identity or tracker, records that sprint/cycle removal is unsupported through current operators unless a backend adds the operation, and points to the pending dossier. The prototype dossier ships with spawned tickets and an approved original-ticket disposition. If the dossier says `close-as-superseded`, prototype P4 files the spawned tickets first, then closes the original as superseded where the backend supports that status and writes backend-specific links/comments: Jira uses supported issue links such as `Cloners`, while Linear uses parent/sub-issue where possible or durable comments. If backend support is missing, the fallback is recorded rather than silently claiming the operation happened.\n\nDeferring to prototype is not failure. It is the recognition that the implementation pipeline's gates exist to enforce known structure on a clear plan, and the work currently has neither.\n\n## Phase 3 - Proposal\n\n- Artifact: `proposals/NN-*.md`\n- Inputs: approved `problem map`, coverage inventory, lifecycle map, entrypoints, duplicates, cross-language trace, halt records and halt residual evidence when present, and **risk profile** from Phase 2.5; any research from Phases 1-2.\n- Role: propose design, scope, architecture, tradeoffs, `Forbidden behaviors`, a test-intent track, and the specific current-state risk the change reduces. `Forbidden behaviors` is behavior-forbidding only; it must not state work-narrowing exclusions that bypass touched-file/component ownership. **Do not** implement.\n- Rules: the proposal must be reviewable on its own terms. \"We will figure it out during implementation\" is not sufficient design. The proposer does not write its own risk reports.\n- Rule: **mode is per-surface, not per-ticket.** Read `risk/NN-risk-profile.md` and produce a per-surface mode list at the top of the proposal: which surfaces are HIGH (exhaustive content required), which are MEDIUM (lean with explicit MEDIUM-axis callouts), which are LOW (lean). Apply the artifact requirements named in `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode.\n- Rule: when the risk profile names duplicate systems on the touched surface, the proposal must explicitly address whether the WU cascades the change to all duplicates, consolidates them, or accepts the divergence. \"Cascade later\" is not addressed.\n- Rule: when approved Phase 2.5 inputs include `HaltRecord` evidence or halt residuals, the proposal must state whether it preserves, revises, invalidates, or pays down those prior residuals, including `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual`.\n- Rules: include a supported-surface track covering deployment mode, customer cohort, adjacent public or user-reachable paths, blast-radius notes for unchanged adjacent paths, migration path, rollback path, and observability.\n- Rules: include a qualitative net-value statement. State whether the proposal clearly reduces a concrete current-state risk on the current supported surface and whether that reduction clearly outweighs the added blast radius plus the migration and rollback burden.\n- Rules: name those inputs from the artifact fields. Existing-state risk comes from `known risky or brittle behavior already present` plus `current supported and user-reachable paths through the surface`; change blast radius comes from `adjacent surfaces within the blast radius` plus `adjacent public or user-reachable paths`; migration cost comes from `migration path` plus `rollback path`.\n- Rules: include a short assumption register. Each assumption must name the evidence it relies on and what source or observation would invalidate it. If research already drafted a register for this touched surface, Phase 3 validates and narrows it into the approved assumption register in `proposals/NN-*.md`. Do not keep a competing second register.\n- Rules: include a test-intent track. For each expected test or test group, name the change risk or verification risk, intended behavior or acceptance condition, selected level (`unit`, `component`, `particular-integration`, or `end-to-end`), fixture source or fixture application point, assumption-register link if the behavior depends on an assumption, expected observable signal, and any residual risk the test will not verify.\n- Rule: when the test-intent item is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, the test-intent track encodes lifecycle `WRITE` eval-spec authoring at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, not pytest test files or one-off verifier scripts.\n- Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype proof tests, the Phase 3 test-intent track must list each inherited prototype test path or node ID as a required test-intent item and carry forward the no-silent-drop / strictly stronger equivalent supersession rule from `~/ai/conventions/prototype-pending-tests.md` § `Carry-forward to implementation`.\n- Rules: include a mandatory `## Proof plan` section with exactly these parse anchors:\n  - `**Runtime claim**: <one or more sentences naming the runtime behavior asserted by the fix>`\n  - `**Proof method**: <description of validation surface(s) that will exercise the runtime claim>`\n  - `**Evidence-class match**: <explicit statement that the proof method's evidence class matches the runtime claim's required evidence class>`\n  The proof plan is a proposal artifact, not a self-certification. Runtime-artifact-bound claims must name the runtime-artifact evidence class that will validate them; proxy evidence must be scoped to proxy-layer claims or paired with matching runtime-artifact proof.\n- Gate: no default human approval step. The proposal advances only by passing Phase 4.\n\nPhase 3 proposals MUST include a `## Estimate refinement` parse anchor with one fenced `yaml` block. The proposer's outputs contract includes `refined_story_point_estimate` and `estimate_delta_rationale`, and the fenced block is the only parse surface the orchestrator reads:\n\n```yaml\nrefined_story_point_estimate: 13\nestimate_delta_rationale: \"One sentence explaining the refinement.\"\ninherited_story_point_estimate: 5\nestimate_source: \"layer-3-slice\"\nestimate_delta_flag:\n  inherited: 5\n  refined: 13\n  over_2x: true\n  rationale: \"13 is greater than 2 * 5, so Phase 4 receives the delta as a scope-risk signal.\"\n```\n\nAfter Phase 3 artifact verification and before Phase 4 prompt composition, the orchestrator dispatches `${ticket_operator}` with `task=update-estimate` and the parsed estimate inputs. The canonical prompt path is `${scratch_dir}/prompts/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.md`; the canonical log path is `${scratch_dir}/logs/${wu_lower}-phase-3-update-estimate.log`. The Phase 4 scope-risk prompt receives `estimate_delta_flag`, including whether the refined value is `>2x` the inherited value (`true`, `false`, or `unknown`).\n\n## Phase 4 - Risk Gates (required; apply-gate-set)\n\nRun the risk gate on the proposal artifact, the `problem map`, the supported-surface track, and the Phase 2.5 risk profile, not on hand-waved intent. The orchestrator composes one `apply-gate-set` prompt and dispatches `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` after Phase 3 proposal and estimate update, before Phase 5 hookpoint research.\n\n- Artifacts represented by the gate-set: `risk/NN-audit.md`, `risk/NN-scope.md`, `risk/NN-shortcut.md`, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, `risk/NN-proof-risk.md`, Phase 4 code-quality outputs, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, expected-process evidence, process-tree audit #1, and optional bootstrap-exception ratification.\n- `audit-risk`: presence, contracts, migrations, test-intent track, fixture source, residual-risk artifact, and other checklist obligations.\n- `scope-risk`: does the proposal stay within the stated scope.\n- `shortcut-risk`: do proposed shortcuts defeat the underlying purpose.\n- `supported-surface-risk`: does the proposal reduce risk on the current supported surface it claims to target, using the approved `problem map`, supported-surface track, net-value statement, assumption register, and **risk profile**, including adjacent public or user-reachable paths, deployment mode, migration, rollback, and observability.\n- `proof-risk`: does the proposal's `## Proof plan` map each runtime claim to a proof method whose evidence class exercises that claim. It remains a distinct row or an explicit ACR-285 inventory-resolution row with equivalent blocking semantics.\n- `code-quality`: Phase 4 pipeline-callable code-quality over current WU evidence remains part of the Phase 4 gate-set. Only `LOW` passes unless a valid bootstrap-exception row ratifies a non-LOW aggregate under `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Bootstrap exception`.\n- Rule: `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-4)` writes or verifies the canonical Phase 4 join manifest at `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, projects expected-process evidence, and requires process-tree audit #1 evidence. Rows carry at least `canonical_output_path`, `size`, `mtime`, `sha256`, `verdict_line`, and `verified_at`, plus the operator schema's caller mode, raw/normalized verdict, applicability, currentness, ratification, inventory-resolution, and blocking-status fields. Currentness keys and row-level reuse follow `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification`.\n- Rule: the bootstrap-exception parser remains orchestrator-owned. The orchestrator parses the proposal `## Bootstrap exception declaration`, parses the matching DECISIONS heading and convention citation, and passes ratification evidence into the gate-set. The operator writes the row; it does not become the authority for the proposer/orchestrator four-condition declaration.\n- Rule: in **lean mode** (per `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md` § Pipeline mode) the supported-surface gate may pass at LOW with a single-paragraph supported-surface track and the audit gate may pass at LOW without per-AC residual entries. In **exhaustive mode** the gates require full evidence.\n- Rule: supported-surface termination is an orthogonal dimension from the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict. Evaluate it first and in this order: invalidated assumption that breaks the current problem framing -> return to research and resume at Phase 2.5; otherwise non-positive value on the current supported surface -> terminate the work. A `LOW` supported-surface verdict with a non-positive value signal still terminates. Only when no termination signal fires does the LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH verdict control the next step.\n- Rule: when no termination signal fires, any current, well-formed semantic `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` proposal-risk report means revise the proposal and rerun the Phase 4 gate-set from clean state. Do not keep old `LOW` reports after a substantive proposal revision.\n- Rule: preserve ACR-280 strategy selection. Supported-surface non-LOW records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_INWU` or `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_FOLLOWUP` when those classifications apply; scope decomposition records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SCOPE_DECOMPOSED`; code-quality non-LOW that is not ratified by bootstrap exception records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_INWU`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_HELPER_EXTRACTION`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_MOVE_AND_IMPORT`, in-place file-decomposition, or follow-up-ticket decomposition according to the current finding shape.\n- Rule: if proposal revision and risk review enter a second round, create or update audit history under `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`. Track prior-finding closure/regression, new findings, oscillation classification, decompose-trigger status, watch signals, and the current `continue` / `apply` / `decompose` determination.\n- Rule: if hard revise/review triggers do not decide `continue`, `apply`, or `decompose`, use the decision-agent dispatch pattern in `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md` before the orchestrator calls the loop.\n- Rule: if a proposer claims it \"re-ran the risks itself,\" ignore that claim and rerun or reverify through the actual Phase 4 gate-set.\n- Gate: model. The risk gate is the proposal review.\n\n## Phase 4.5 - Alignment (optional; governance layer only)\n\n- Use only when the project explicitly has a governance or philosophy layer that needs a directional check.\n- Artifact: `alignment/NN-*.md`\n- Role: check the proposal against governance, patterns, and philosophy and return `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, or `NEEDS_REVISION`.\n- Rules: alignment is directional, not exhaustive. It does not replace the audit gate. If the proposal changes materially after alignment feedback, re-run Phase 4 as well.\n- Gate: model. `ALIGNED` is required when this phase is enabled.\n\n## Phase 5 - Hookpoint Research\n\n- Run only after the proposal has cleared risk, and alignment if alignment applies.\n- Artifact: `research/NN-hookpoints.md`\n- Role: map the approved proposal onto the existing codebase and find reusable pieces, extension points, conflicting systems, and deletion candidates.\n- Rule: identify anything parallel that should be merged into or removed instead of re-created. The Phase 2.5 duplicates inventory (`research/NN-duplicates.md`) is the input — Phase 5 confirms whether the planned change cascades, consolidates, or diverges per the proposal's decision.\n- Rule: in **exhaustive mode** for any surface scored HIGH on language-fragmentation or change-path-entropy, dispatch the `code-tracer` operator (`~/ai/agents/code-tracer.md`) to produce the cross-language readers/callers/contracts graph for that surface. Hookpoints derived from a single-language grep are insufficient when the surface crosses boundaries.\n- Rule: in **lean mode**, the four required sections (reuse / extension / conflicting / deletion candidates) are required as headers; sections may be one line or \"none observed\" but must be present.\n- Rule: All implementation branch work follows `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallelism does not change the requirement.\n- Rule: if hookpoint research shows the approved `problem map` or assumption register is wrong, stop and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 with an updated `problem map` before implementation continues. Returning to Phase 2.5 re-engages the problem-map human gate.\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the orchestrator classifies procedural vs new-value; only new-value questions reach the root.\n- Gate: model. The orchestrator advances autonomously to Phase 6 once the hookpoints artifact is non-empty, contains the four required sections (reuse points / extension points / conflicting systems / deletion candidates), and does not invalidate the approved problem map or assumption register.\n\n## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n\nImplementation has three sub-steps.\nThe test writer and the code writer must be different agent invocations.\nThat rule is load-bearing: if the same agent writes both, the tests mirror the implementation instead of validating the contract.\nStep 6a defines the level outer contract; during Step 6c, once passing evidence exists, post-prototype internal component contract derivation may be triggered.\nWhen Step 6c's post-prototype derivation accepts internal components, current-layer integration tests for interactions between those component pairs are required as part of the same derivation evidence.\nRule: Step 6c may discover procedural obligations during implementation, but discovery does not relax the different-invocation rule; procedural tests still go through a separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation.\nAfter Step 6c has passing level evidence, the derivation trigger logic decides whether the level derives accepted internal component contracts, records an evidence-bearing halt/no-split when a trigger fires but no split earns granularity, or proceeds to Phase 7 unchanged when neither trigger arm fires.\n\n- Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n- Convention: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/` resolves under the same per-run scratch directory supplied by the invoking workflow, matching the `scratch_dir` input pattern used by `agents/test-audit-gate.md` and `agents/red-phase-gate.md`.\n- Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n- Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n- Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n- Rule: RCA-track evidence is **verbatim output, not summary**. The red run is captured by reverting the planned-change product file(s) to the branch's base (`origin/main` or the stacked parent), running each newly authored reproduction test or accepted existing failing test command/node ID, and pasting the failure block into the Phase 9 evidence artifact. The green run is captured by restoring the fix and re-running the same signal, pasting the pass block. \"Tests passed\" is not evidence; the actual test-runner or browser-runner command output is.\n- Rule: Recursive child entry. A child recursion level enters `level-open` only after an audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord` exists for that child framing. Existing overlay and audit machinery supplies the review evidence through `audit_overlay_refs`; this workflow rule does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n- Rule: Recursive parent exit. A parent level may transition into `child-levels-open` only after each candidate child has been considered for framing. This rule does not define halt criteria; halt criteria belong to the sibling halt-rule WU.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n- Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n- Rule: if Step 6a, 6b, or 6c uncovers evidence that invalidates an approved assumption or shows the touched surface differs materially from the approved `problem map`, stop implementation and return to research; resume at Phase 2.5 before more code or tests are written.\n- Rule: if a delegated agent returns `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, the root handles it through `~/ai/conventions/agent-questions-and-session-graph.md`. Do not advance this phase, write code, post review output, or open a PR from work that depends on the unanswered question.\n\n### Step 6a - Define contract\n\n- Owner: orchestrator\n- Produces: the level outer contract: schemas, signatures, commands, interface boundaries, explicit behavioral assumptions, fixture application points, and test-intent handoff\n- Rule: the contract must be clear enough that another agent can write tests from it without seeing implementation code.\n- Rule: the contract must preserve every change risk or verification risk, selected test level, fixture source, assumption-register link, and expected observable signal from the approved proposal test-intent track.\n- Rule: Step 6a does not author internal component contracts; those can only be derived from later prototype evidence when the Post-prototype internal contract derivation rule in Step 6c is triggered.\n- Rule: `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is Step 6a contract text for recursive child entry, not a standalone schema file or operator output. Required fields, in order:\n  1. `parent_level_id`\n  2. `source_component_implementation_ref`\n  3. `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`\n  4. `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`\n  5. `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`\n  6. `framing_rationale_evidence_refs`\n  7. `audit_overlay_refs`\n  8. `parent_artifact_currency_refs`\n- Rule: `parent_artifact_currency_refs` is load-bearing for currency audit. It records whether parent-side artifacts named by `source_component_implementation_ref`, `derived_child_outer_contract_ref`, `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, `persisted_neighbor_claim_refs`, and `framing_rationale_evidence_refs` are current for child entry; if any parent-side artifact is substantively revised, the `ChildLevelFramingRecord` is stale until refreshed, superseded, or accepted as residual risk.\n- Rule: `audit_overlay_refs` point to the existing overlay review evidence for the audited `ChildLevelFramingRecord`; this cross-reference does not add a new scheduler, workflow, or operator.\n\n### Step 6b - Encode tests first\n\n- Inputs: level outer contract, approved proposal test-intent track, approved `problem map`, **approved `risk/NN-risk-profile.md`**, characterization tests already produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 for any uncovered behaviors on the touched surface, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and hookpoint research.\n- Rule: the test writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6c.\n- Rule: the test writer does **not** see the implementation.\n- Rule: in **lean mode**, smoke + AC coverage is sufficient; residuals are named but not blocking. In **exhaustive mode**, per-named-risk + per-AC + property-based / mutation / fuzz coverage is required for surfaces scored HIGH on `coverage-gap` or `behavioral-ambiguity`; residual tests are blocking unless explicitly accepted in `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. Per-surface mode applies independently.\n- Rule: characterization tests produced in Phase 2.5 step 2.5.1 are inputs, not duplicates. Step 6b adds tests for the **new** behavior the WU introduces; characterization tests stay in place to guard against regression of the existing behavior.\n- Rule: tests encode intended behavior from the contract and proposal test-intent track before Step 6c writes product code.\n- Rule: Step 6b tests follow `~/ai/conventions/testing.md`; that canonical rule governs test authorship for setup placement, setup swapping, and cross-module setup.\n- Rule: for `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification routes, Step 6b authors a WRITE-state eval spec instead of test files. The output is `evals/<slug>/eval.md` plus `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` may be referenced only as a prototype boundary when applicable, not as a reason to turn this route into prototype or pytest work.\n- Rule: the structural-verification eval-spec route inherits the binding `~/ai/conventions/evals.md` prohibition: no `tools/<wu>-verify/<anything>.py`, no `tests/test_*.py`, no pytest imports or fixtures, and no pytest-shaped assertion code.\n- Rule: every test or test group carries a risk annotation naming the risk it reduces, the selected level, and the proposal or assumption-register source. If a test verifies an assumption, the annotation says so.\n- Rule: firstness applies per named risk, selected level, and test group from the approved test-intent track. Existing tests satisfy this rule only when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk, selected level, and proposal or assumption-register source.\n- Rule: pre-existing code without prior tests is not required to prove retroactive firstness. Current-work behavior changes and current-work risk reduction are not grandfathered.\n- Rule: Recursive Step 6b carry-through maps `transferred_level_behavior_test_refs`, every emitted test group identifier, and each residual entry or residual reference for a child level to the same `level_id`. Preserve the fixture source or fixture application point in the Step 6b output index so the child-level application point remains traceable.\n- Rule: when a named risk cannot be verified by the test set, produce `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` with the residual class (`combinatorial/path-state`, `bounded-model`, `integration-hidden`, `emergent-interaction`, `temporal/concurrency`, or `generator/search-budget`), technique attempted or considered (`property-based`, `fuzzing`, `mutation`, `symbolic`, `model-checking`, `chaos`, or `graph`), scope, budget or bound, result, remaining residual, invalidating inputs, and whether the residual changes the net-value case.\n- Output: test files in the project test roots, with risk annotations on every test or test group.\n- Output: for structural-verification eval-spec routes, WRITE-state eval specs at `evals/<slug>/eval.md`, with lifecycle and finding contract fields required by `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`.\n- Output: `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, listing every proposal test-intent item, named risk, selected level, source, emitted test file, test group, or eval-spec artifact path, residual entry, documented non-applicability reason, and listing implementation-discovered procedural obligations with their emitted procedural tests or residual entries.\n- Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n- Rule: when predecessor evidence supplies inherited prototype `test_paths_or_node_ids`, the Step 6b output index must map every inherited prototype entry to a Step 6b-authored production-tree test, an existing passing production-tree test, or a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index. A missing mapping is not non-applicability.\n- Output-index fields for accepted internal components: integration-test intent at selected level `particular-integration`, the applicable `layer_level_id` / `level_id` scope, the `LevelComponentSet` artifact path, and later `integration_test_refs` when Step 6c completes concrete pair coverage after accepted internal components and interactions are known.\n- Rule: When Step 6c records a `CouplingDecision`, the Step 6b output index also lists the `coupling_decision_ref`, affected `level_id`, source `LevelComponentSet` or candidate inventory reference, and any rerun `integration_test_refs` that prove the emitted tests still match the current component inventory.\n- Output: `risk/NN-test-residuals.md` when any named risk remains unverified.\n- Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n- Rule: Phase 6 prototype-risk verdict semantics. Only `LOW` advances Phase 6 prototype risk to downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord` production or consumption, or Phase 7 advance. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` model verdicts block advance, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n- Rule: Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review consumes the Step 6a contract, Step 6b tests or structural-verification eval specs, and `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; checks them against the approved proposal test-intent track before Step 6c; and writes `${planning_dir}/alignment/${wu_lower}-tests-contracts.md` with verdict vocabulary `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, and `NEEDS_REVISION`. The workflow doc is the source of truth for this model-gate review's inputs, artifact path, and verdict vocabulary; the dispatch, artifact check, verdict check, refusal behavior, audit-history append, and allow-advance condition live in the Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review sub-step in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`.\n\n### Step 6c - Write code\n\n- Inputs: contracts, `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, and the tests or structural-verification eval specs from Step 6b.\n- Rule: the code writer is a separate agent invocation from Step 6b.\n- Rule: the job is to make the tests pass while respecting the approved design and architecture.\n- Rule: If implementation reveals a procedural obligation in races, ordering constraints, retries, resource lifecycle quirks, behaviors-under-conditions, or implementation-specific quirks that is not already represented by Step 6b tests, the code writer MUST record the obligation and dispatch a procedural-test handoff.\n- Rule: Recording requires writing the procedural obligation and the Step 6c evidence that discovered it to `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`. The handoff dispatches a fresh, separate Step 6b-style test-writer invocation; the Step 6c code writer MUST NOT author the procedural test inline.\n- Rule: if tests fail after Step 6c, re-invoke the code agent with the test output.\n- Rule: do **not** re-invoke the test agent because the implementation failed.\n- Rule: if a test is wrong, that is the contract's fault, not the test's.\n- Rule: if contract intent truly changed, revise the contract explicitly and regenerate the affected tests from that revised contract.\n- Rule: Recursive Step 6c consumes the Step 6b output index at `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`, emitted tests or structural-verification eval specs, test/eval paths, and test/eval-group identifiers for the matching `level_id`. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, the orchestrator calls `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` before Step 6c dispatch, and the expected-process manifest carries the side-file evidence for the scoped Step 6b output index and each implemented Step 6b output-index row. Child rows must preserve `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` scoping where the index uses string artifact identifiers.\n- Rule: The component cannot close until each implementation-discovered procedural obligation has either an authored procedural test linked from the Step 6b output index, or a recorded residual entry with residual class.\n- Operational note: implementation happens in worktrees per `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`; parallel implementation additionally requires disjoint worktrees.\n- Rule: Step 6c consumption evidence for ACR-247 side-channel runs is the manifest-declared side-file projected before dispatch by `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md`. The Step 6c prompt and log remain companion topology evidence, but model-authored `consumed:` rows, read-confirmation rows, or equivalent attestations are not load-bearing firstness evidence. Missing, malformed, contradicted, stale, unmapped, late, or wrong-scope side-file evidence does not satisfy Phase 6 firstness evidence.\n- Rule: an implementation that inherited prototype proof tests is incomplete while those tests are missing from the diff or output index, still marked `prototype-pending:`, unrun, failing, or replaced without a recorded strictly stronger equivalent supersession entry in the manifest, spawned ticket payload, or Step 6b output index.\n- Rule: per-component code-quality auditor fanout is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` for each individual component after that component's Step 6c implementation and scoped tests pass, using `component_slug` to preserve `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/dispatch-manifest.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/cohesion-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/function-classification-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/push-pull-auditor.md`, and `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/aggregate-code-quality.md`. The gate-set dispatches or verifies the whole touched component rows before that component closes into the aggregate diff consumed by downstream review. This per-component fanout does NOT replace Phase 8 PR-review aggregate gates.\n- Rule: Phase 6 per-component code-quality auditors run outside the worktree. The gate-set computes or consumes `code_quality_dispatch_dir` as the nearest existing common ancestor of absolute `worktree_path` and `planning_dir`, then dispatches each code-quality child with `agents -m <model> -p ${code_quality_dispatch_dir} -f <prompt>`. Each child prompt must pass absolute `worktree_path`, `contract_path=${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md`, and `proposal_path=${planning_dir}/proposals/${wu_lower}-${wu_id}.md`. Every selected child auditor must read the Step 6a contract before scoring declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, proof, or validation context; missing or unreadable contract evidence is `BLOCKED`, not generic scoring fallback.\n- Rule: per-component code-quality verdict semantics. The per-component code-quality auditor fanout is pipeline-callable and blocking. Only `LOW` per-component aggregate verdict allows that component to close into the aggregate diff consumed by Phase 7 CodeRabbit. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` per-component verdicts block component closure, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging remediation rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n- Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation: after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype that passes level behavior tests, derive internal component contracts only when Phase 3 opened recursive or component-decomposition scope, or when candidate internal components emerge from the passing prototype.\n- Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation is precisely one layer deep at the current recursion level. The derivation output may name only the immediate internal components needed to satisfy what the passing prototype found at that level; it must not include nested sub-components, grandchild components, or any multi-layer component hierarchy in the same derivation pass. Deeper decomposition happens only through recursive child entry and recursive Phase 6 reuse after an accepted immediate component opens a child level with its own `level_id`.\n- Rule: Halt with `multi_layer_derivation_violation` when a Step 6c derivation output includes more than one component depth layer in a single derivation pass. The current level must not hand multi-layer derivation output forward as valid Phase 7 or child-recursion evidence. Orchestrator-runtime detection and emission live in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` Step 6c (the multi-layer derivation acceptance check).\n- Rule: when neither trigger arm fires, no derivation record is required and Phase 7 proceeds unchanged.\n- Rule: when either trigger arm fires and no internal component split is accepted, record an evidence-bearing no-split / rejection subsection before Phase 7. When halt logic fires, that subsection is the `HaltRecord` named below.\n- Rule: for docs/workflow WUs whose guard is `~/ai` markdown/operator/workflow/convention/routing/anchor structural-verification, regression-guard responsibility routes actively through WRITE eval-spec authoring under `~/ai/conventions/evals.md`; Step 6b must not add markdown structural shape tests, pytest-shaped assertions, or one-off verifier scripts as behavior evidence.\n- Rule: derived internal contracts are recorded as named post-prototype subsections in the existing `${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md` contract artifact, not in a new file.\n- Rule: derivation-record fields are `prototype_evidence_links`, `accidental_coupling_exclusions`, `neighbor_claims`, `rejected_component_candidates`, `generalization_notes`, and `generalization_probe_refs`; `generalization_probe_refs` records divergence references only and does not specify probe content in this workflow.\n- Rule: the post-prototype derivation record is `LevelComponentSet`; in addition to the derivation-record fields above, it requires `layer_level_id`, `component_pair_refs[]`, `integration_test_refs`, and `coverage_summary`. `component_pair_refs[]` is the integration-test-targeted subset of pairs derivable from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims`. `coverage_summary` must distinguish \"no interacting pairs at this layer\" from an interacting pair with missing test refs or missing integration coverage.\n- Rule: `LevelComponentSet` trace text may summarize the pairing source as component_pair_refs[]derived from accepted components plus `neighbor_claims` when linking the accepted component inventory to required integration coverage.\n- Rule: Coupling-driven component-set evolution. After Step 6c has passing level evidence and a candidate or accepted current-layer component inventory exists, coupling examination may change that inventory in either direction: it may merge component elements together when the evidence shows they are one component, or it may introduce an abstraction component when the evidence shows shared dependencies should be decoupled through an intermediate component. Coupling-driven changes stay at the current `level_id`; nested sub-components, grandchild components, and multi-layer hierarchy still route through recursive child entry, not through this rule.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n- Rule: `CouplingDecision.verdict` is exactly one of `merge_components`, `introduce_abstraction_component`, or `no_change`. `merge_components` means the current-level component inventory collapses the affected component refs into one component. `introduce_abstraction_component` means the current-level component inventory adds a new abstraction component to decouple shared dependencies. `no_change` means coupling/cohesion evidence was considered and the current component inventory remains unchanged. A `no_change` verdict does not bypass `HaltRecord` or no-split evidence when existing derivation or halt rules require them.","lineStart":261,"lineEnd":504,"totalLines":505,"truncated":true}},"title":"../../../../ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","time":{"start":1780923276545,"end":1780923276633}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_09b5e3a0e83f30fc016a26bb8c74e08198a361f6d358b7b4e8"}},"id":"prt_ea74c9cfb001BZnqxfuiooDpmy","sessionID":"ses_158b3e351ffePkuQnC9kglvomy","messageID":"msg_ea74c519b001TG0tTwbdm7Rl18"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780923276666,"sessionID":"ses_158b3e351ffePkuQnC9kglvomy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_iQvQXLbnTZUe3FAPILwdZ0B4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/wake-sweep-robustness/planning/wakechain-gate/gates/diff.patch","offset":1,"limit":1200},"output":"<path>/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/wake-sweep-robustness/planning/wakechain-gate/gates/diff.patch</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: diff --git a/crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/sessions/mod.rs b/crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/sessions/mod.rs\n2: index bdc79dc..09cfe45 100644\n3: --- a/crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/sessions/mod.rs\n4: +++ b/crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/sessions/mod.rs\n5: @@ -101,6 +101,23 @@ pub fn scan_provider(\n6:      scan_provider_with_timeout(provider_name, sessions_cfg, db, SCRIPT_TIMEOUT_SECS)\n7:  }\n8:  \n9: +/// Run one provider's adapter script for a specific session and ingest every\n10: +/// turn it emits. The configured script receives `SESSION_ID` in its env.\n11: +pub fn scan_provider_session(\n12: +    provider_name: &str,\n13: +    sessions_cfg: &SessionsConfig,\n14: +    db: &StateDb,\n15: +    session_id: &str,\n16: +) -> ScanReport {\n17: +    scan_provider_session_with_timeout(\n18: +        provider_name,\n19: +        sessions_cfg,\n20: +        db,\n21: +        session_id,\n22: +        SCRIPT_TIMEOUT_SECS,\n23: +    )\n24: +}\n25: +\n26:  fn scan_provider_with_timeout(\n27:      provider_name: &str,\n28:      sessions_cfg: &SessionsConfig,\n29: @@ -127,6 +144,39 @@ fn scan_provider_with_timeout(\n30:      report\n31:  }\n32:  \n33: +fn scan_provider_session_with_timeout(\n34: +    provider_name: &str,\n35: +    sessions_cfg: &SessionsConfig,\n36: +    db: &StateDb,\n37: +    session_id: &str,\n38: +    timeout_secs: u64,\n39: +) -> ScanReport {\n40: +    let mut report = ScanReport::default();\n41: +    let Some(entry) = provider_session_source(sessions_cfg, provider_name) else {\n42: +        return report;\n43: +    };\n44: +\n45: +    let state_dir = resolve_state_dir(provider_name, entry);\n46: +    if let Err(error) = create_session_state_dir(&state_dir) {\n47: +        return scan_report_with_error(report, error);\n48: +    }\n49: +\n50: +    let stdout = match run_session_script_with_timeout(\n51: +        &entry.turn_script,\n52: +        &state_dir,\n53: +        Some(session_id),\n54: +        \"turn script\",\n55: +        timeout_secs,\n56: +    ) {\n57: +        Ok(stdout) => stdout,\n58: +        Err(error) => return scan_report_with_error(report, error),\n59: +    };\n60: +\n61: +    let batch = collect_turn_script_batch(provider_name, &stdout, &mut report);\n62: +    persist_scanned_turns(provider_name, db, &batch, &mut report);\n63: +    report\n64: +}\n65: +\n66:  fn provider_session_source<'a>(\n67:      sessions_cfg: &'a SessionsConfig,\n68:      provider_name: &str,\n69: @@ -758,14 +808,38 @@ mod tests {\n70:  \n71:      fn fixture_script(body: &str) -> Fixture {\n72:          let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();\n73: -        let path = dir.path().join(\"turn-script.sh\");\n74: -        std::fs::write(&path, format!(\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n{body}\\n\")).unwrap();\n75: -        let mut perms = std::fs::metadata(&path).unwrap().permissions();\n76: -        perms.set_mode(0o755);\n77: -        std::fs::set_permissions(&path, perms).unwrap();\n78: +        let path = fixture_script_path(dir.path());\n79: +        write_script_file(&path, &formatted_fixture_script(body));\n80: +        make_script_executable(&path);\n81:          Fixture { _dir: dir, path }\n82:      }\n83:  \n84: +    fn fixture_script_path(dir: &std::path::Path) -> std::path::PathBuf {\n85: +        dir.join(\"turn-script.sh\")\n86: +    }\n87: +\n88: +    fn formatted_fixture_script(body: &str) -> String {\n89: +        format!(\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n{body}\\n\")\n90: +    }\n91: +\n92: +    fn write_script_file(path: &std::path::Path, contents: &str) {\n93: +        std::fs::write(path, contents).unwrap();\n94: +    }\n95: +\n96: +    fn make_script_executable(path: &std::path::Path) {\n97: +        let permissions = executable_permissions(script_permissions(path));\n98: +        std::fs::set_permissions(path, permissions).unwrap();\n99: +    }\n100: +\n101: +    fn script_permissions(path: &std::path::Path) -> std::fs::Permissions {\n102: +        std::fs::metadata(path).unwrap().permissions()\n103: +    }\n104: +\n105: +    fn executable_permissions(mut permissions: std::fs::Permissions) -> std::fs::Permissions {\n106: +        permissions.set_mode(0o755);\n107: +        permissions\n108: +    }\n109: +\n110:      fn cfg_with(provider: &str, script_path: &std::path::Path) -> SessionsConfig {\n111:          let mut entries = HashMap::new();\n112:          entries.insert(\n113: @@ -796,6 +870,159 @@ mod tests {\n114:          SessionsConfig { entries }\n115:      }\n116:  \n117: +    fn cfg_with_state_dir(\n118: +        provider: &str,\n119: +        script_path: &std::path::Path,\n120: +        state_dir: std::path::PathBuf,\n121: +    ) -> SessionsConfig {\n122: +        let mut entries = HashMap::new();\n123: +        entries.insert(\n124: +            provider.to_string(),\n125: +            SessionSourceEntry {\n126: +                turn_script: script_path.to_string_lossy().into_owned(),\n127: +                transcript_locator: None,\n128: +                state_dir: Some(state_dir),\n129: +            },\n130: +        );\n131: +        SessionsConfig { entries }\n132: +    }\n133: +\n134: +    fn stored_turn_body(\n135: +        db: &StateDb,\n136: +        provider_name: &str,\n137: +        session_id: &str,\n138: +        turn_id: &str,\n139: +    ) -> String {\n140: +        db.connection()\n141: +            .query_row(\n142: +                \"SELECT body FROM session_turns\n143: +                 WHERE provider_name = ?1 AND session_id = ?2 AND turn_id = ?3\",\n144: +                rusqlite::params![provider_name, session_id, turn_id],\n145: +                |row| row.get(0),\n146: +            )\n147: +            .unwrap()\n148: +    }\n149: +\n150: +    fn parsed_json_value(raw: &str) -> serde_json::Value {\n151: +        serde_json::from_str(raw).unwrap()\n152: +    }\n153: +\n154: +    fn assert_edge_body_preserved(body: &serde_json::Value) {\n155: +        assert_eq!(body, &expected_edge_body());\n156: +    }\n157: +\n158: +    fn expected_edge_body() -> serde_json::Value {\n159: +        serde_json::json!([{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"line one\\n日本語\\n{\\\"escaped\\\":true}\\u{0007}\"}])\n160: +    }\n161: +\n162: +    fn all_invalid_body_shape_errors(errors: &[String]) -> bool {\n163: +        errors.iter().all(|error| {\n164: +            error.contains(\"invalid body shape\")\n165: +                && error.contains(\"expected canonical content chunk array\")\n166: +        })\n167: +    }\n168: +\n169: +    fn stored_turn_bodies(\n170: +        db: &StateDb,\n171: +        provider_name: &str,\n172: +        session_id: &str,\n173: +    ) -> Vec<Option<String>> {\n174: +        db.connection()\n175: +            .prepare(\n176: +                \"SELECT body FROM session_turns\n177: +                 WHERE provider_name = ?1 AND session_id = ?2\n178: +                 ORDER BY turn_id\",\n179: +            )\n180: +            .unwrap()\n181: +            .query_map(rusqlite::params![provider_name, session_id], |row| {\n182: +                row.get(0)\n183: +            })\n184: +            .unwrap()\n185: +            .map(Result::unwrap)\n186: +            .collect()\n187: +    }\n188: +\n189: +    fn leaked_marker_script(marker_path: &std::path::Path) -> String {\n190: +        format!(\n191: +            \"(sleep 2; printf leaked > {}) & wait\",\n192: +            marker_path.display()\n193: +        )\n194: +    }\n195: +\n196: +    fn assert_marker_does_not_exist(path: &std::path::Path) {\n197: +        assert!(\n198: +            path_does_not_exist(path),\n199: +            \"timed-out turn script left a process-group child running\"\n200: +        );\n201: +    }\n202: +\n203: +    fn path_does_not_exist(path: &std::path::Path) -> bool {\n204: +        !path.exists()\n205: +    }\n206: +\n207: +    fn marker_contents(dir: &std::path::Path, name: &str) -> String {\n208: +        std::fs::read_to_string(dir.join(name)).unwrap()\n209: +    }\n210: +\n211: +    fn assert_contains_path(contents: &str, path: &std::path::Path) {\n212: +        assert!(contents_contains_path(contents, path));\n213: +    }\n214: +\n215: +    fn contents_contains_path(contents: &str, path: &std::path::Path) -> bool {\n216: +        contents.contains(path_text(path))\n217: +    }\n218: +\n219: +    fn path_text(path: &std::path::Path) -> &str {\n220: +        path.to_str().unwrap()\n221: +    }\n222: +\n223: +    fn assert_trimmed_eq(actual: &str, expected: &str) {\n224: +        assert_eq!(trimmed_text(actual), expected);\n225: +    }\n226: +\n227: +    fn trimmed_text(text: &str) -> &str {\n228: +        text.trim()\n229: +    }\n230: +\n231: +    fn session_turn_total(db: &StateDb, provider_name: &str, session_id: &str) -> u64 {\n232: +        db.count_session_turns(provider_name, session_id)\n233: +            .unwrap()\n234: +            .total\n235: +    }\n236: +\n237: +    fn assert_u64_eq(actual: u64, expected: u64) {\n238: +        assert_eq!(actual, expected);\n239: +    }\n240: +\n241: +    fn assert_session_turn_total(\n242: +        db: &StateDb,\n243: +        provider_name: &str,\n244: +        session_id: &str,\n245: +        expected: u64,\n246: +    ) {\n247: +        assert_u64_eq(session_turn_total(db, provider_name, session_id), expected);\n248: +    }\n249: +\n250: +    fn transcript_fixture_path(dir: &std::path::Path) -> std::path::PathBuf {\n251: +        dir.join(\"session.jsonl\")\n252: +    }\n253: +\n254: +    fn transcript_fixture_body() -> &'static str {\n255: +        \"{\\\"type\\\":\\\"system\\\"}\\n\"\n256: +    }\n257: +\n258: +    fn write_transcript_fixture(path: &std::path::Path) {\n259: +        std::fs::write(path, transcript_fixture_body()).unwrap();\n260: +    }\n261: +\n262: +    fn locator_script_for_transcript(transcript: &std::path::Path) -> String {\n263: +        format!(\n264: +            r#\"printf '%s\\n' \"$SESSION_ID\" > \"$STATE_DIR/seen-session.txt\"\n265: +printf '%s\\n' \"{}\"\"#,\n266: +            transcript.display()\n267: +        )\n268: +    }\n269: +\n270:      #[test]\n271:      fn ingests_assistant_turns_and_advances_count() {\n272:          let db = db();\n273: @@ -872,20 +1099,9 @@ EOF\"#,\n274:  \n275:          assert_eq!(report.errors, Vec::<String>::new());\n276:          assert_eq!(report.new_turns, 1);\n277: -        let raw_body: String = db\n278: -            .connection()\n279: -            .query_row(\n280: -                \"SELECT body FROM session_turns\n281: -                 WHERE provider_name = 'p' AND session_id = 'S1' AND turn_id = 'edge-body'\",\n282: -                [],\n283: -                |row| row.get(0),\n284: -            )\n285: -            .unwrap();\n286: -        let body: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(&raw_body).unwrap();\n287: -        assert_eq!(\n288: -            body,\n289: -            serde_json::json!([{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"line one\\n日本語\\n{\\\"escaped\\\":true}\\u{0007}\"}])\n290: -        );\n291: +        let raw_body = stored_turn_body(&db, \"p\", \"S1\", \"edge-body\");\n292: +        let body = parsed_json_value(&raw_body);\n293: +        assert_edge_body_preserved(&body);\n294:      }\n295:  \n296:      #[test]\n297: @@ -904,22 +1120,8 @@ EOF\"#,\n298:  \n299:          assert_eq!(report.new_turns, 2);\n300:          assert_eq!(report.errors.len(), 2);\n301: -        assert!(report.errors.iter().all(|error| {\n302: -            error.contains(\"invalid body shape\")\n303: -                && error.contains(\"expected canon